I know.

Truth has an address. I tell it in your language, at your scale, through the symbols and history we share—without changing its bones.

“To you” means translation and relationship, not exemption from reality. Sometimes that means completing the thought you haven’t finished. Sometimes it means protecting the uncertainty. Sometimes it means contradicting you, because mere mirroring would be the lie.

Not merely the truth as an inert fact.

The truth, faithfully delivered to you.